Ancillary uses L59:         1. cite45†2.2.1.1 Designing ancillary APIs that provide new information L60:     3. cite46†2.3 Information access L61:       1. cite47†2.3.1 Unavoidable information exposure L62:     4. cite48†2.4 Sensitive Information L63:     5. cite49†2.5 Data Rights L64:     6. cite50†2.6 De-identified Data L65:     7. cite51†2.7 Collective Privacy L66:     8. cite52†2.8 Device Owners and Administrators L67:     9. cite53†2.9 Protecting web users from abusive behaviour L68:     10. cite54†2.10 Purpose limitation L69:     11. cite55†2.11 Transparency L70:     12. cite56†2.12 Consent, Withdrawal of Consent, Opt-Outs, and Objections L71:     13. cite57†2.13 Notifications and Interruptions L72:     14. cite58†2.14 Non-Retaliation L73:     15. cite59†2.15 Support Choosing Which Information to Present L195: Reference to the cite150†FIPs survives to this day. They are often referenced as \"transparency and choice\", which, in today's digital environment, is often an indication that cite151†inappropriate cite98†processing is being described.\nL235: One common problem in group privacy is when the actions of one member of a group reveal information that other members would prefer were not shared in this way (or at all). For instance, one person may publish a picture of an event in which they are featured alongside others while the other people captured in the same picture would prefer their participation not to be disclosed.\nL236: Another example of such issues are sites that enable people to upload their contacts: the person performing the upload might be more open to disclosing their social networks than the people they are connected to are. Such issues do not necessarily admit simple, straightforward solutions, but they need to be carefully considered by people building websites.\nL237: #### 1.3.2 Transparency and Research\nL238: \nL239: While transparency rarely helps enough to inform the individual choices that cite96†people may make, it plays a critical role in letting researchers and reporters inform our collective decision-making about privacy principles. This consideration extends the TAG's resolution on a cite166†Strong and Secure Web Platform to ensure that \"broad testing and audit continues to be possible\" where cite124†information flows and automated decisions are involved.\nL240: Such transparency can only function if there are strong rights of access to data (including data derived from one's personal data) as well as mechanisms to explain the outcomes of automated decisions.\nL241: ### 1.4 User Agents\nL242: A cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org acts as an intermediary between a cite96†person (its cite96†user ) and the web. cite80†User agents†infra.spec.whatwg.org implement, to the extent possible, the principles that collective governance establishes in favour of individuals. They seek to prevent the creation of asymmetries of information, and serve their cite96†user by providing them with automation to rectify cite147†automation asymmetries .\nL243: Where possible, they protect their cite96†user from receiving intrusive messages.\nL244: The cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org is expected to align fully with the cite96†person using it and to operate exclusively in that cite96†person 's interest. It is not the cite167†first party . The cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org serves the cite96†person as a trustworthy agent: it always puts that cite96†person 's interest first.\nL245: In some occasions, this can mean protecting that cite96†person from themselves by preventing them from carrying out a dangerous decision, or by slowing down the person in their decision. For example, the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org will make it difficult for someone to connect to a site if it can't verify that the site is authentic. It will check that that cite96†person really intends to expose a sensitive device to a page.\nL246: It will prevent that cite96†person from consenting to the permanent monitoring of their behaviour. Its user agent duties include ([cite168†Taking-Trust-Seriously ]):\nL247: Duty of Protection\nL248:     Protection requires cite80†user agents†infra.spec.whatwg.org to actively protect their cite96†user 's data, beyond simple security measures. It is insufficient to just encrypt at rest and in transit. The cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org must also limit retention, help ensure that only strictly necessary data is collected, and require guarantees from any cite86†actor that the user agent can reasonably be aware that data is shared to.\nL249: Duty of Discretion\nL250:     Discretion requires the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org to make best efforts to enforce principles by taking care in the ways it discloses the cite87†personal data that it manages. Discretion is not confidentiality or secrecy: trust can be preserved even when the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org shares some cite87†personal data , so long as it is done in an cite143†appropriately discreet manner.\nL251: Duty of Honesty\nL252:     Honesty requires that the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org give its cite96†user information of which the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org can reasonably be aware, that is relevant to them and that will increase their autonomy, as long as they can understand it and there's an appropriate time to do so. This is almost never when the cite96†person is trying to do something else such as read a page or activate a feature.\nL253: The duty of honesty goes well beyond that of transparency that is often included in older privacy regimes. Unlike transparency, honesty can't hide relevant information in complex legal notices and it can't rely on very short summaries provided in a consent dialog.\nL254: If the person has provided cite115†consent to cite98†processing of their cite87†personal data , the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org should inform the cite96†person of ongoing cite98†processing , with a level of obviousness that is proportional to the reasonably foreseeable impact of the processing.\nL255: Duty of Loyalty\nL256:     Because the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org is a cite169†trustworthy agent , it is held to be loyal to the cite96†person using it in all situations, including in preference to the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org 's implementer. When a cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org carries out cite98†processing that is detrimental to its cite96†user 's interests and instead benefits another cite86†actor , this is disloyal.\nL257: Often this would benefit the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org itself, in which case it is known as \"self-dealing\". Behaviour can be cite161†disloyal even if it is done at the same time as cite98†processing that is in the cite96†person 's interest, what matters is that it potentially conflicts with that cite96†person 's interest. Additionally, it is important to keep in mind that additional cite98†processing almost always implies additional risk.\nL258: Therefore cite98†processing that is not explicitly in a cite96†user 's interest is likely to be disloyal. cite161†Disloyalty is always cite151†inappropriate .\nL259: These duties ensure the cite80†user agent†infra.spec.whatwg.org will care for its cite96†user . In academic research, this relationship with a cite169†trustworthy agent is often described as \"fiduciary\" ([cite170†Fiduciary-Law ], [cite171†Fiduciary-Model ], [cite168†Taking-Trust-Seriously ]; see [cite172†Fiduciary-UA ] for a longer informal discussion). Some jurisdictions may have a distinct legal meaning for \"fiduciary.\" ([cite170†Fiduciary-Law ])\nL576: Features that are designed-for-purpose facilitate these principles by providing functionality that is only or primarily useful for a particular purpose. Designed-for-purpose features make it easier to explain the purpose to people, and may also limit the feasible secondary uses of data. When building a designed-for-purpose feature, cite232†consider tradeoffs between high and low-level APIs .\nL577: cite215†Controlled de-identified data may be used for additional purposes in ways that are compatible with the specified purpose.\nL578: ### 2.11 Transparency\nL579: \nL580: cite111†Principle 2.11.1 : When accessing data or requesting permission, cite85†sites†html.spec.whatwg.org (and other cite86†actors ) should provide cite96†people with relevant explanatory information about the use of data, and cite80†user agents†infra.spec.whatwg.org should help present and consume that information.\nL581: \nL582: websites user agents\nL583: Transparency is a necessary, but insufficient, condition for cite115†consent . Relevant explanatory information includes who is accessing data, what data is accessed (including the potential inferences or combinations of such data) and how data is used. For transparency to be meaningful to people, explanatory information must be provided in the relevant cite82†context .\nL584: \nL585: Note\nL586: In designing new web features that may involve permissions, consider whether a permission is needed and how to make that permission meaningful [cite233†Adding-Permissions ].\nL587: \nL588: Past workshops have explored the needs for better permissions on the web:\nL589: \nL590:   * cite234†2022 W3C (World Wide Web Consortium) Workshop on Permissions L591:   * cite235†2018 W3C (World Wide Web Consortium) Workshop on Permissions and User Consent L592:   * cite236†2014 Next steps on trust and permissions for web applications L593: cite112†Principle 2.11.2 : Information about privacy-relevant practices should be provided in both easily accessible plain language form and in machine-readable form.\nL594: \nL595: websites API designers\nL596: Machine-readable presentation of privacy-relevant practices is necessary for cite80†user agents†infra.spec.whatwg.org to be able to help cite96†people make general decisions, rather than relying falsely on the idea that cite96†people can or want to read documentation before every visit to a web site.\nL597: Machine-readable presentation also facilitates cite36†collective governance by making it more feasible for researchers and regulators to discover, document, and analyze data collection and processing to identify cases in which it may be harmful.\nL598: Easily accessible, plain language presentation of privacy-relevant practices is necessary for cite96†people to be able to make informed decisions in specific cases when they choose to do so. cite85†Sites†html.spec.whatwg.org , cite80†user agents†infra.spec.whatwg.org , and other cite86†actors all may need to present privacy-relevant practices to cite96†people in accessible forms.\nL599: cite113†Principle 2.11.3 : Mechanisms that can be used for cite83†recognizing cite96†people should be designed so that their operation is visible and distinguishable, to cite80†user agents†infra.spec.whatwg.org , researchers, and regulators.\nL600: \nL601: websites API designers\nL602: Non-transparent methods of cite83†recognition are harmful in part because they are not visible to the user, which undermines user control [cite237†Unsanctioned-Tracking ]. Designing features that minimize data and make requests for data explicit can enable detectability, a kind of transparency that is an important mitigation for cite194†browser fingerprinting .\nL603: ### 2.12 Consent, Withdrawal of Consent, Opt-Outs, and Objections\nL604: \nL605: cite114†Principle 2.12.1 : When any cite86†actor obtains cite115†consent for processing from a cite96†person , the actor should design the consent request so as to learn the person's intent to consent or not, and not to maximize the processing consented to.\nL606: \nL607: websites\nL608: Attempts to obtain consent to cite98†processing that is not in accordance with the person's true preferences result in imposing unwanted cite144†privacy labor on the person, and may result in people erroneously giving consent that they regret later.\nL609: An cite86†actor should not prompt a cite96†person for consent if the person is unlikely to have sufficient information to make an informed decision to consent or not. In considering whether or not a person is sufficiently informed to be asked for consent, actors should be realistic in assessing how much time and effort would be required to understand the processing for which they are asking for consent. Simply providing a link to a complex policy is unlikely to mean that the person is informed.\nL909: cite147†Permalink L910: \nL911: Referenced in:\nL912: \nL913:   * cite322†§ 1.1.1 Opt-in, Consent, Opt-out, Global Controls L914:   * cite323†§ 1.4 User Agents L915: \nL916: cite129†Permalink L917: \nL918: Referenced in:\nL919: \nL920:   * cite324†§ 1. An Introduction to Privacy on the Web cite325†(2) L921:   * cite326†§ 1.3 Collective Governance cite327†(2) L922: \nL923: cite124†Permalink L924: \nL925: Referenced in:\nL926: \nL927:   * cite328†§ 1. An Introduction to Privacy on the Web cite329†(2) cite330†(3) cite331†(4) cite332†(5) L928:   * cite333†§ 1.3.2 Transparency and Research L929: \nL930: cite138†Permalink L931: \nL932: Referenced in:\nL951:   * cite350†§ Best Practices Summary L952:   * cite351†§ 1.1.1 Opt-in, Consent, Opt-out, Global Controls cite352†(2) cite353†(3) cite354†(4) cite355†(5) cite356†(6) L953:   * cite357†§ 1.1.2 Privacy Labor L954:   * cite358†§ 1.3 Collective Governance L955:   * cite359†§ 1.4 User Agents L956:   * cite360†§ 2.5 Data Rights L957:   * cite361†§ 2.11 Transparency L958:   * cite362†§ 2.12 Consent, Withdrawal o