cite754†(5) cite755†(6) cite756†(7) cite757†(8) cite758†(9) cite759†(10) cite760†(11) L1393:   * cite949†§ Best Practices Summary cite950†(2) L1394:   * cite951†§ 2.1 Identity on the Web cite952†(2) cite953†(3) cite954†(4) cite955†(5) L1395:   * cite956†§ 2.2 Data Minimization L1396:   * cite957†§ 2.11 Transparency cite958†(2) L1397:   * cite959†§ A.5 Recognition cite960†(2) L1398:   * cite961†§ A.5.1 Recognition Types cite962†(2) cite963†(3) cite964†(4) cite965†(5) cite966†(6) cite967†(7) cite968†(8) cite969†(9) L1399:   * cite970†§ A.5.2 User agent awareness of recognition L1400: \nL1401: cite195†Permalink L1402: \nL1403: Referenced in:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n<details> HTML details disclosure element - HTML | MDN (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/details)\nciteturn3view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"pattern\":\"space\"}); Total lines: 714\nL241: The `name` attribute specifies a group name — give multiple `<details>` elements the same `name` value to group them. Only one of the grouped `<details>` elements can be open at a time — opening one will cause another to close. If multiple grouped `<details>` elements are given the `open` attribute, only the first one in the source order will be rendered open.\nL242: \nL243: Note: `<details>` elements don't have to be adjacent to one another in the source to be part of the same group.\nL244: ## cite92†Usage notes L245: \nL246: A `<details>` widget can be in one of two states. The default closed state displays only the triangle and the label inside `<summary>` (or a cite99†user agent -defined default string if no `<summary>`).\nL247: \nL248: When the user clicks on the widget or focuses it then presses the space bar, it \"twists\" open, revealing its contents. The common use of a triangle which rotates or twists around to represent opening or closing the widget is why these are sometimes called \"twisty\".\nL249: You can use CSS to style the disclosure widget, and you can programmatically open and close the widget by setting/removing its cite97†`open` attribute. Unfortunately, at this time, there's no built-in way to animate the transition between open and closed.\nL250: \nL251: By default when closed, the widget is only tall enough to display the disclosure triangle and summary. When open, it expands to display the details contained within.\nL252: Fully standards-compliant implementations automatically apply the CSS `cite100†`display` : list-item` to the cite88†`<summary>` element. You can use this or the cite101†`::marker` pseudo-element to cite102†customize the disclosure widget .\nL253: ### cite103†Events L254: \nL255: In addition to the usual events supported by HTML elements, the `<details>` element supports the cite104†`toggle` event, which is dispatched to the `<details>` element whenever its state changes between open and closed. It is sent after the state is changed, although if the state changes multiple times before the browser can dispatch the event, the events are coalesced so that only one is sent.\nL256: \nL257: You can use an event listener for the `toggle` event to detect when the widget changes state:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDisclosure (Show/Hide) Pattern | APG | WAI | W3C (https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/disclosure/)\nciteturn3view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view3\",\"pattern\":\"Enter\"}); Total lines: 69\nL22: A disclosure is a widget that enables content to be either collapsed (hidden) or expanded (visible). It has two elements: a disclosure cite10†button and a section of content whose visibility is controlled by the button. When the controlled content is hidden, the button is often styled as a typical push button with a right-pointing arrow or triangle to hint that activating the button will display additional content. When the content is visible, the arrow or triangle typically points down.\nL23: \nL24: cite11†Image L25: ## Examples\nL26: \nL27:   * cite12†Disclosure (Show/Hide) of Image Description L28:   * cite13†Disclosure (Show/Hide) of Answers to Frequently Asked Questions L29:   * cite14†Disclosure (Show/Hide) Navigation Menu L30:   * cite15†Disclosure (Show/Hide) Navigation Menu with Top-Level Links L31:   * cite16†Disclosure (Show/Hide) Card L32: ## Keyboard Interaction\nL33: \nL34: When the disclosure control has focus:\nL35: \nL36:   * `Enter`: activates the disclosure control and toggles the visibility of the disclosure content.\nL37:   * `Space`: activates the disclosure control and toggles the visibility of the disclosure content.\nL38: ## WAI-ARIA Roles, States, and Properties\nL39: \nL40:   * The element that shows and hides the content has role cite17†button†w3c.github.io .\nL41:   * When the content is visible, the element with role `button` has cite18†aria-expanded†w3c.github.io set to `true`. When the content area is hidden, it is set to `false`.\nL42:   * Optionally, the element with role `button` has a value specified for cite19†aria-controls†w3c.github.io that refers to the element that contains all the content that is shown or hidden.\nL43: ## Help improve this page\nL44: \nL45: Please share your ideas, suggestions, or comments via e-mail to the publicly-archived list public-aria-practices@w3.org or via GitHub.\nL46: \nL47: E-mail (public-aria-practices@w3.org)cite20†Fork & Edit on GitHub†github.com cite21†New GitHub Issue†github.com L48: \nL49: cite22†Back to Top L50: \nL51: cite23†W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI) L52: \nL53: Strategies, standards, resources to make the Web accessible to people with disabilities\nL54:   * cite24†Image ci